'''topside''' (top′sīd), ''n.'' [< ''top''<sup>1</sup> + ''side''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. The top side; the upper part. {{smaller|Usually as two words, ''top side'', except in the specific use (def. 2), and in the expressions ''topside-turned'', ''topside-turvy'', ''topside-turvied'', and the phrases following, all being accommodated forms of ''topsy-turvy'' (which see).}}

2. Specifically, the upper part of a ship's sides; the side of a ship above the water-line: commonly in the plural.

{{smaller block|She had not strained a single butt or rivet in her ''topsides''.
{{right|''Sci. Amer. Supp.'', p. 8777.}}}}

'''Topside the other way†, topside tother way†, topside turfway†.''' {{smaller|Same as ''topsyturvy'', of which these phrases are sophisticated amplifications, suggesting a false derivation.}}

{{smaller block|The estate of that flourishing towne was turned . . . ''topside the otherwaie'', and from abundance of prosperitie quite exchanged to extreame penurie.
{{right|''Stanihurst'', Descrip. of Ireland, iii. (Holinshed's Chron., I.)}}}}

{{smaller block|Thus were all things strangely turned in a trice ''topside t'other way'': they who lately were confined as prisoners are now not only free, but petty Lords and Masters, yea and petty Kings.
{{right|''H. L'Estrange'', Reign of K. Charles (ed. 1655), p. 75.}}}}

